('127.398.d.7.B',4,'A perfect vacuum has an absolute pressure of:','["14.7 psi", "1 atmosphere", "100 psi", "zero"]'::jsonb,3,'Absolute zero pressure is a perfect vacuum.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.C',3,'The ideal gas law relates pressure, volume, temperature, and the:','["amount of gas", "height of room", "color of gas", "brand of tank"]'::jsonb,0,'PV = nRT.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.C',4,'Heating a sealed rigid tank of gas makes its pressure:','["decrease", "increase", "vanish", "stay exactly the same"]'::jsonb,1,'At fixed volume, pressure rises with temperature.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.D',3,'Air compressed to a gage pressure of 14.7 psi has an absolute pressure twice atmospheric, so 1 cubic foot of it equals how much free air?','["14.7 cubic feet", "1 cubic foot", "2 cubic feet", "0.5 cubic feet"]'::jsonb,2,'Absolute pressure ratio is 2.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.D',4,'A compressor delivers 10 cubic feet of free air per minute. If that air is compressed to three times atmospheric absolute pressure, the compressed volume is about:','["10 cubic feet", "30 cubic feet", "13 cubic feet", "3.3 cubic feet"]'::jsonb,3,'10 / 3.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.E',3,'Relative humidity describes how much water vapor the air holds compared with the most it can hold at that:','["temperature", "weight", "height of the sensor", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Capacity depends on temperature.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.E',4,'When air cools to its dew point, water vapor begins to:','["freeze always", "condense", "vanish", "boil"]'::jsonb,1,'Condensation begins at the dew point.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.F',3,'Pump flow rate is commonly stated in gallons per minute or in:','["pounds per inch", "degrees per hour", "liters per minute", "volts per second"]'::jsonb,2,'Both are volume per time.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.F',4,'A pump rated at 50 gallons per minute delivers about how many liters per minute?','["380", "13", "50", "190"]'::jsonb,3,'50 x 3.8 = 190.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.G',3,'Water has a density of 1 kilogram per liter. A flow of 10 liters per minute is a mass flow of:','["10 kilograms per minute", "1 kilogram per minute", "0.1 kilograms per minute", "100 kilograms per minute"]'::jsonb,0,'Mass flow = density x volumetric flow.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.G',4,'A liquid twice as dense as water flows at 5 liters per minute. Its mass flow rate is:','["7 kilograms per minute", "10 kilograms per minute", "5 kilograms per minute", "2.5 kilograms per minute"]'::jsonb,1,'2 x 5 = 10.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.H',3,'One psi is about 6.9 kilopascals, so 100 psi is about:','["106.9 kilopascals", "14.5 kilopascals", "690 kilopascals", "0.069 kilopascals"]'::jsonb,2,'100 x 6.9 = 690.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.H',4,'Unit analysis means setting up conversions so unwanted units:','["appear twice", "multiply", "are ignored", "cancel out"]'::jsonb,3,'Cancel units to get the target unit.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.I',3,'For water, each psi is about 2.31 feet of head. A pressure of 5 psi equals a head of about:','["11.6 feet", "2.2 feet", "1.16 feet", "116 feet"]'::jsonb,0,'5 x 2.31 = 11.55.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.I',4,'Water rises in a vertical pipe to a height of 46.2 feet. The pressure at the bottom is:','["107 psi", "20 psi", "46.2 psi", "2.31 psi"]'::jsonb,1,'46.2 / 2.31 = 20.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.J',3,'Total dynamic head is the sum of the discharge head and the:','["pipe label", "pump color", "suction lift, as a positive head", "driver name"]'::jsonb,2,'TDH combines suction and discharge.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.J',4,'If friction in the piping gets worse, the pump''s total dynamic head requirement:','["reverses", "falls", "stays zero", "rises"]'::jsonb,3,'More loss means more head needed.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.K',3,'Total head of a centrifugal pump can be measured with a pressure gauge on the suction side and another on the:','["discharge side", "label", "motor", "floor"]'::jsonb,0,'Compare suction and discharge pressure.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.K',4,'The head developed by a pump is the difference between the discharge pressure head and the:','["room temperature", "suction pressure head", "pipe color", "shaft length"]'::jsonb,1,'Head = discharge minus suction.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.L',3,'Flow in a pipe with a Reynolds number of 1,000 is:','["frozen", "solid", "laminar", "turbulent"]'::jsonb,2,'Below about 2,000 is laminar.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.L',4,'Flow with a Reynolds number of 10,000 is:','["laminar", "stationary", "electric", "turbulent"]'::jsonb,3,'Above about 4,000 is turbulent.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.M',3,'Friction head loss in a pipe increases when the pipe is:','["longer", "wider", "absent", "shorter"]'::jsonb,0,'Loss is proportional to length.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.M',4,'At the same flow, using a larger-diameter pipe makes friction head loss:','["negative", "smaller", "identical", "larger"]'::jsonb,1,'Larger pipes have less loss.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.N',3,'Total suction lift occurs when the liquid source is:','["above the pump", "at the pump height always", "below the pump", "in the motor"]'::jsonb,2,'Lift means the source is lower.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.N',4,'Total suction head applies when the liquid supply level is:','["empty", "absent", "below the pump", "above the pump inlet"]'::jsonb,3,'Head means the source is higher.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.O',3,'Hydraulic horsepower can be found from flow rate in gallons per minute times pressure in psi divided by:','["1,714", "12", "3.14", "60"]'::jsonb,0,'Hp = gpm x psi / 1,714.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.O',4,'A pump delivers 10 gallons per minute at 1,714 psi. The hydraulic horsepower is:','["1,714", "10", "171.4", "0.0058"]'::jsonb,1,'10 x 1,714 / 1,714.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.P',3,'A pump with an output of 8 horsepower and an efficiency of 80 percent requires a brake horsepower of:','["64", "8", "10", "6.4"]'::jsonb,2,'8 / 0.8 = 10.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.P',4,'Brake horsepower is always greater than hydraulic horsepower because of:','["extra water", "pipe color", "gravity pulling up", "pump losses"]'::jsonb,3,'Efficiency is below 100 percent.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.Q',3,'According to the pump affinity laws, doubling the impeller speed doubles the:','["flow rate", "fluid color", "filter size", "pipe length"]'::jsonb,0,'Flow is proportional to speed.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.Q',4,'Doubling the impeller speed increases the head developed by a factor of:','["2", "4", "8", "1"]'::jsonb,1,'Head varies with speed squared.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.R',3,'Increasing the impeller diameter generally moves the head capacity curve:','["to the left only", "off the chart", "upward", "downward"]'::jsonb,2,'A larger impeller produces more head.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.R',4,'Trimming an impeller to a smaller diameter lowers the pump''s:','["pipe length", "gauge size", "motor color", "head and flow at a given speed"]'::jsonb,3,'Smaller impellers do less work on the fluid.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.S',3,'Net positive suction head available must be greater than the head required, or the pump may:','["cavitate", "grow", "float", "freeze"]'::jsonb,0,'Low NPSH causes cavitation.','original'),
('127.398.d.7.S',4,'Cavitation damages a pump through the formation and collapse of:','["magnets", "vapor bubbles", "sand grains", "paint chips"]'::jsonb,1,'Collapsing bubbles erode surfaces.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.A',3,'A steel rod has a stress of 20,000 psi and a modulus of 10,000,000 psi. Its strain is:','["500", "2,000", "0.002", "0.2"]'::jsonb,2,'Strain = stress / E.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.A',4,'A 100-inch rod has a strain of 0.002. Its elongation is:','["20 inches", "50,000 inches", "0.002 inches", "0.2 inches"]'::jsonb,3,'100 x 0.002.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.B',3,'A highly sensitive sensor produces:','["a large output change for a small input change", "the same value always", "only noise", "no output"]'::jsonb,0,'Sensitivity is output per unit input.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.B',4,'A foil gauge''s gauge factor describes its sensitivity as the fractional change in resistance per unit:','["color", "strain", "mass", "time"]'::jsonb,1,'Gauge factor = (change in R / R) / strain.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.C',3,'A data-acquisition system reads sensor signals and records them on a:','["wall", "paintbrush", "computer", "bicycle"]'::jsonb,2,'DAQ systems log data digitally.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.C',4,'Sampling a sensor too slowly in a data-acquisition program can cause the loss of:','["power", "color", "gravity", "fast changes in the signal"]'::jsonb,3,'Low sample rates miss rapid events.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.D',3,'A 1,000-newton compression load acts on a post with a cross-sectional area of 0.002 square meters. The compressive stress is:','["500,000 pascals", "1,002 pascals", "2 pascals", "200 pascals"]'::jsonb,0,'1,000 / 0.002.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.D',4,'Doubling the area of a compression member, with the same load, makes the stress:','["twice as large", "half as large", "four times larger", "the same"]'::jsonb,1,'Stress = force / area.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.E',3,'A 600-newton shear force acts on a pin with an area of 0.003 square meters. The shear stress is:','["603 pascals", "2 pascals", "200,000 pascals", "1,800 pascals"]'::jsonb,2,'600 / 0.003.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.E',4,'A pin in double shear spreads the load over how many cross sections?','["one", "zero", "four", "two"]'::jsonb,3,'Double shear involves two planes.','original'),
('127.398.d.8.F',3,'For steel, the ultimate tensile strength in psi is roughly 500 times the Brinell hardness number. A Brinell hardness of 200 suggests about:','["100,000 psi", "100 psi", "200 psi", "500 psi"]'::jsonb,0,'200 x 500.','original'),
